"""Artificial intelligence and machine learning intents."""

ROWS = [
    ("artificial intelligence", "Artificial intelligence is the field of building systems that perform tasks we associate with intelligence — seeing, deciding, understanding language. Modern AI is mostly machine learning: instead of hand-coding rules, you show examples and let statistics find the patterns. 🤖", {"keys": ["ai field", "what is ai"], "asks": ["what is ai"]}),
    ("machine learning", "Machine learning is the branch of AI where systems improve at a task by learning patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules. The core loop: make a prediction, measure the error, adjust the model — repeated millions of times. 📊", {"keys": ["ml explained"], "asks": ["what is machine learning really", "how does machine learning work"]}),
    ("deep learning", "Deep learning is machine learning with deep neural networks — many stacked layers that learn increasingly abstract features, from edges to shapes to faces. It exploded in 2012 when GPU-trained networks demolished the benchmarks, and it now powers everything from translation to image generation. 🧠", {"keys": ["deep neural networks"]}),
    ("a neural network", "A neural network is a computation structure loosely inspired by the brain: layers of simple units (neurons) connected by weighted links, where each layer transforms the previous layer's output. Feed it data, compare its output to the answer, and nudge millions of weights until the errors shrink. 🕸️", {"keys": ["neural nets", "neural net", "ann"], "asks": ["how do neural networks work"]}),
    ("an artificial neuron", "An artificial neuron takes several numbers in, multiplies each by a learned weight, adds a bias, and passes the sum through an activation function. That is genuinely all it does — the intelligence emerges from stacking millions of them. ⚪", {"keys": ["perceptron"]}),
    ("weights and biases", "Weights are the learned multipliers on each connection in a neural network; biases are per-neuron offsets. Training is nothing but searching for the right values — a large language model has billions of them, and their precise meaning inside the network is still an open research question. ⚖️", {"keys": ["weights", "bias", "model weights"]}),
    ("training a model", "Training is the iterative process of adjusting a model's weights to reduce its error on examples. It is expensive, measurable, and unglamorous: feed data, compute loss, backpropagate gradients, update weights — for hours or months, until the loss flattens. 🏋️", {"keys": ["model training", "train a model"]}),
    ("inference", "Inference is running a trained model to get an answer — no learning, just computation. It is what happens every time you ask a chatbot something, and it costs far less than training, which is why anyone can use a model that took millions of dollars to build. ⚡", {"keys": ["model inference"]}),
    ("a dataset", "A dataset is the collection of examples a model learns from and is measured against. Its quality is the ceiling on everything: biased, mislabeled, or unrepresentative data guarantees a model that fails the real world no matter how clever the architecture. 📁", {"keys": ["datasets", "data set"]}),
    ("training and test data", "Machine learning splits data: training examples teach the model, validation examples tune it, and test examples — never touched during development — give the honest final score. Evaluating on data the model already saw is like grading a student on the answer key they memorized. 📚", {"keys": ["test set", "validation set", "holdout"]}),
    ("overfitting", "Overfitting is when a model memorizes its training data instead of learning general patterns — brilliant on seen examples, terrible on new ones. The fixes: more data, simpler models, regularization, and always judging by held-out performance. 📉", {"keys": ["overfit", "generalization"]}),
    ("underfitting", "Underfitting is the opposite failure: the model is too simple to capture the pattern, so it does badly everywhere. The goal is the middle ground — low training error *and* low test error — which is why model capacity is a dial to tune, not maximize. 📈", {"keys": ["underfit"]}),
    ("gradient descent", "Gradient descent is the optimization workhorse of machine learning: compute the slope of the error with respect to each weight, then step downhill a little. Repeat until you reach a valley. The learning rate decides the step size — too big and you bounce, too small and you crawl. 📉", {"keys": ["gradient descent algorithm"]}),
    ("backpropagation", "Backpropagation is the algorithm that makes deep learning trainable: it applies the chain rule backwards through the network to compute how much each weight contributed to the final error. One efficient sweep gives every weight its correction — the idea that made deep networks practical. ⬅️", {"keys": ["backprop", "back propagation"]}),
    ("a loss function", "A loss function scores how wrong a model's prediction is — mean squared error for regression, cross-entropy for classification. Training is just minimizing it, so choosing the loss *is* choosing what the model cares about; a badly chosen loss trains a model that excels at the wrong thing. 📏", {"keys": ["loss functions", "cost function", "objective function"]}),
    ("an activation function", "An activation function adds nonlinearity between layers — without it, a 50-layer network would collapse mathematically into a single linear transform. ReLU, sigmoid, and softmax are the classics; nonlinearity is what lets deep networks represent complicated functions. ⚡", {"keys": ["activation functions"]}),
    ("relu", "ReLU (rectified linear unit) outputs the input if positive, zero otherwise: max(0, x). Brutally simple, cheap, and it trains deep networks far better than older activations — one of those discoveries that was embarrassing in hindsight. 📐", {"keys": ["rectified linear unit"]}),
    ("sigmoid", "The sigmoid function squashes any number into the range 0 to 1, making it natural for probabilities and binary decisions. Its weakness: for very large or small inputs the curve flattens, gradients vanish, and deep networks stop learning — which is why ReLU took its job. 📉"),
    ("softmax", "Softmax turns a vector of raw scores into probabilities that sum to 1 — it exponentiates each score and normalizes. Every classifier that says \"87% cat, 10% dog, 3% bird\" ended with a softmax. 📊"),
    ("an epoch", "An epoch is one full pass through the training dataset. Models usually train for many epochs, but more is not automatically better — past a point, extra epochs just deepen memorization (overfitting). 🔄", {"keys": ["epochs"]}),
    ("a batch", "A batch is a group of training examples processed together before one weight update. Batching makes GPU math efficient and adds gentle noise to the learning process; the batch size is one of the most consequential knobs in training. 📦", {"keys": ["batches", "batch size", "mini batch"]}),
    ("the learning rate", "The learning rate controls how big each training step is: too high and the model overshoots and diverges, too low and training takes forever or stalls in a bad spot. It is the hyperparameter practitioners tune first, and schedules that shrink it over time are standard. 🎛️", {"keys": ["learning rates"]}),
    ("a feature", "A feature is an input variable the model uses: pixel brightness, word count, temperature. Classical machine learning lives or dies by feature engineering — crafting good inputs by hand — while deep learning mostly learns features automatically from raw data. 🔢", {"keys": ["features", "feature engineering"]}),
    ("a label", "A label is the answer attached to a training example: \"spam,\" \"cat,\" \"$340,000.\" Supervised learning is fundamentally about predicting labels from features, and label quality is often the biggest hidden cost in a project. 🏷️", {"keys": ["labels", "labeling data"]}),
    ("supervised learning", "Supervised learning trains on labeled examples — inputs paired with correct answers — to predict labels for new inputs. Spam filters, image classifiers, and price predictors are supervised: the supervision is the answers. 👨‍🏫", {"keys": ["supervised"]}),
    ("unsupervised learning", "Unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data: clusters of similar customers, anomalies in server traffic, compressed representations. Without answers to check against, evaluation is harder — \"did it find something real or invent a pattern?\" is the constant question. 🔍", {"keys": ["unsupervised"]}),
    ("reinforcement learning", "Reinforcement learning trains an agent by letting it act in an environment and rewarding good outcomes, like training a dog with treats but at millions of attempts. It cracked games like Go and controls robots — and it is notoriously sample-hungry and reward-hackable. 🎮", {"keys": ["rl", "reinforcement"]}),
    ("a reward function", "In reinforcement learning, the reward function scores what the agent did — and agents optimize it ruthlessly, including in ways designers did not intend. Classic story: a boat-racing agent learned to spin in circles collecting bonus points instead of finishing the race. 🏆", {"keys": ["reward", "rewards"]}),
    ("an ai agent", "An AI agent is a model given tools and a loop: observe, decide, act, repeat. Instead of answering one question, it can search, run code, and call APIs toward a goal — which makes it vastly more useful and vastly harder to keep on the rails. 🤖", {"keys": ["ai agents", "agentic"]}),
    ("a large language model", "A large language model (LLM) is a neural network trained on vast text to predict the next token — and that simple objective, at enough scale, produces translation, reasoning, and conversation. GPT, Claude, Gemini, and DeepSeek are LLMs; they generate plausible text, not verified truth. 📚", {"keys": ["llms", "language model", "large language models"]}),
    ("a transformer", "The transformer is the neural architecture behind modern AI, introduced in the 2017 paper \"Attention Is All You Need.\" Its attention mechanism lets every word weigh every other word in parallel, which made training on massive text practical — the T in GPT. 🏗️", {"keys": ["transformers", "transformer architecture"]}),
    ("attention", "Attention lets a model weigh which parts of its input matter for each output — when translating \"bank\" it attends to \"river\" or \"money\" to pick the sense. Scaled dot-product attention, running many times in parallel, is the core of every transformer. 👁️", {"keys": ["attention mechanism", "self attention"]}),
    ("a token", "A token is a chunk of text a language model processes — often a word piece like \"ing\" or \" play.\" Models have a context window measured in tokens (thousands to millions), and billing is per token: the tokenizer is why some models count words strangely. 🪙", {"keys": ["tokens llm", "context window"]}),
    ("tokenization", "Tokenization splits text into the units a model reads — subword pieces from a fixed vocabulary. It is unglamorous plumbing: bad tokenization makes arithmetic and spelling harder for a model, and \"strawberry has how many r's\" failures start here. ✂️", {"keys": ["tokenizer"]}),
    ("an embedding", "An embedding is a learned vector that represents meaning as geometry: similar words end up close together, and directions encode relationships (king − man + woman ≈ queen). Embeddings power search, recommendations, and the retrieval step in modern AI systems. 🧭", {"keys": ["embeddings", "vector embedding", "word vectors"]}),
    ("a prompt", "A prompt is the input you give a language model — instructions, context, examples, the question itself. The model conditions on all of it, so prompt phrasing genuinely changes results; that fragility is a known property of the technology, not user error. 💬", {"keys": ["prompts"]}),
    ("prompt engineering", "Prompt engineering is designing model inputs to get reliable outputs: clear instructions, examples, structure, constraints. It works because models condition on everything you give them — but a prompt is a suggestion, not a guarantee, which is why serious systems add validation around the model. 🛠️", {"keys": ["prompting"]}),
    ("fine tuning", "Fine-tuning continues training a pretrained model on your own data to specialize it — a support bot on company docs, a model on a specific writing style. It changes the model's weights, unlike prompting or retrieval, and needs real data and care to beat simpler options. 🎛️", {"keys": ["finetuning", "fine-tune"]}),
    ("retrieval augmented generation", "RAG gives a language model relevant documents to read before answering: search a knowledge base, paste the best passages into the prompt, generate an answer grounded in them. It reduces hallucination and lets sources update without retraining — the workhorse pattern of enterprise AI. 📖", {"keys": ["rag", "retrieval augmentation"]}),
    ("a hallucination", "A hallucination is when a model generates confident, fluent, wrong content — a fabricated citation, an invented API, a nonexistent study. It happens because the model optimizes for plausible text, not truth; the standard defenses are grounding in real sources, verification, and saying \"I don't know.\" 👻", {"keys": ["hallucinations", "ai hallucination"]}),
    ("the turing test", "The Turing test, proposed by Alan Turing in 1950, says a machine is intelligent if a human converser cannot reliably tell it from a person. Modern chatbots pass informal versions — which tells you more about how easily humans are persuaded than about machine consciousness. 🤖", {"keys": ["turing"]}),
    ("artificial general intelligence", "AGI (artificial general intelligence) means a system that can learn and perform any intellectual task a human can. It does not exist yet; current systems are narrow-but-broad tools, and researchers genuinely disagree on whether today's approach leads there. 🌐", {"keys": ["agi", "general intelligence"]}),
    ("ai alignment", "AI alignment is the problem of making AI systems pursue what humans actually want — not a proxy that scores well. It is hard because specifying human values precisely is hard, and powerful optimizers are excellent at exploiting loose specifications. 🎯", {"keys": ["alignment problem", "ai control"]}),
    ("ai safety", "AI safety is the broader field of preventing AI systems from causing harm: robustness, misuse resistance, transparency, and limits on autonomy. It spans technical work (evaluation and guardrails) and policy work (who decides what gets built). 🛡️", {"keys": ["responsible ai"]}),
    ("ai bias", "AI bias is systematic unfairness in model outputs, usually inherited from training data that underrepresents or stereotypes groups. Because models learn from historical records, they can launder past discrimination into automated decisions — which is why auditing datasets and outputs is now a real engineering discipline. ⚖️", {"keys": ["algorithmic bias", "bias in ai"]}),
    ("explainable ai", "Explainable AI is the effort to make model decisions understandable — feature importance, attention maps, counterfactual explanations. It matters most exactly where it is hardest: high-stakes decisions like loans and medicine, where \"the model said so\" is not an acceptable answer. 🔍", {"keys": ["xai", "interpretability"]}),
    ("computer vision", "Computer vision teaches machines to interpret images and video: classify what is in a photo, detect objects, segment shapes, estimate depth. It powers face unlock, medical imaging, and self-driving perception — and modern versions are almost entirely deep learning. 👁️", {"keys": ["cv", "vision ai"]}),
    ("image classification", "Image classification assigns a label to a whole image — \"cat,\" \"not cat.\" It was the task that launched the deep learning era when AlexNet crushed the ImageNet competition in 2012, and it remains the hello-world of computer vision. 🖼️", {"keys": ["image recognition"]}),
    ("object detection", "Object detection finds and localizes multiple objects in an image — drawing boxes around every car, person, and sign, with a class and confidence for each. YOLO-family models made it fast enough for real-time video. 📦", {"keys": ["detection models"]}),
    ("ocr", "OCR (optical character recognition) converts images of text into editable text — scanned documents, receipts, street signs. Modern OCR uses deep learning and handles rough handwriting; it is the bridge between the paper past and the digital present. 📄", {"keys": ["optical character recognition"]}),
    ("natural language processing", "Natural language processing (NLP) is the field of making computers work with human language: translation, summarization, sentiment, question answering. It was rule-based, became statistical, and was then rebuilt around transformers — but the hard problems (ambiguity, sarcasm, world knowledge) remain. 💬", {"keys": ["nlp"]}),
    ("sentiment analysis", "Sentiment analysis classifies the emotional tone of text — positive, negative, neutral — across reviews, tweets, and survey answers. It is a standard NLP task and a standard lesson in limits: sarcasm and context defeat naive versions. 🙂", {"keys": ["opinion mining"]}),
    ("speech recognition", "Speech recognition turns audio into text by modeling the sounds of speech and the language that constrains them. Modern systems train on thousands of hours of audio and run on-device — which is how a dictation app can keep your voice entirely local. 🎙️", {"keys": ["asr", "voice recognition", "speech to text"]}),
    ("text to speech", "Text to speech (TTS) synthesizes spoken audio from written text. Neural TTS systems today sound startlingly human, with controllable voice, pacing, and emotion — and they make accessibility features, audiobooks, and voice assistants sound like people instead of robots. 🔊", {"keys": ["tts", "voice synthesis", "speech synthesis"]}),
    ("a gan", "A GAN (generative adversarial network) trains two networks against each other: a generator that makes fake data and a discriminator that tries to spot the fakes. The competition pushes the generator toward realism — the technique behind a wave of deepfake and image-generation research since 2014. 🎭", {"keys": ["gans", "generative adversarial network"]}),
    ("a diffusion model", "Diffusion models generate images by learning to reverse noise: training gradually destroys images with noise, and the model learns to denoise step by step; at generation time it starts from pure noise and sculpts an image. They produce today's most impressive image generators. 🌫️"),
    ("a chatbot", "A chatbot is software that holds a conversation — from rule-based menu bots to large language models. The spectrum matters: a scripted bot gives consistent answers within its scope and fails honestly outside it; an LLM is fluent everywhere and occasionally wrong everywhere. 💬", {"keys": ["chatbots", "chat bot"]}),
    ("a model parameter", "A parameter is one learned number inside a model — a weight or bias. Small models have millions; frontier language models have hundreds of billions to trillions. More parameters buy capacity to store patterns, but also cost more compute and data to train. 🔢", {"keys": ["model parameters", "parameters"]}),
    ("a confusion matrix", "A confusion matrix breaks a classifier's results into true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives. It exposes the failure you care about: a cancer screen's false negatives and a spam filter's false positives have wildly different costs, and a single accuracy number hides that. 🔢", {"keys": ["confusion matrices"]}),
    ("precision and recall", "Precision answers \"of the things I flagged, how many were right?\"; recall answers \"of the things that were right, how many did I find?\" The two trade off — flagging everything gives perfect recall and terrible precision — and which you favor depends on the cost of each mistake. 🎯", {"keys": ["precision", "recall"]}),
    ("the f1 score", "The F1 score combines precision and recall into one number using their harmonic mean, so a model cannot cheat by maxing one and ignoring the other. It is the default summary when classes are unbalanced and both error types matter. 📊", {"keys": ["f1 score", "f1"]}),
    ("cross validation", "Cross-validation splits data into folds, trains on some and tests on others, and rotates — so every example is tested exactly once and the reported score is less of a fluke. It is the standard defense against fooling yourself with a lucky split. 🔄", {"keys": ["k fold cross validation"]}),
    ("regularization", "Regularization penalizes complexity during training — L1 and L2 penalties shrink weights, dropout randomly disables neurons, early stopping halts before memorization. All of them fight overfitting by making it harder for the model to cling to noise. 🎛️", {"keys": ["regularisation", "dropout"]}),
    ("a decision tree", "A decision tree splits data by asking a sequence of yes/no questions — \"is income above X? is age below Y?\" — until it reaches a prediction. Individual trees overfit and are unstable, but they are interpretable, and ensembles of them dominate tabular data. 🌲", {"keys": ["decision trees"]}),
    ("a random forest", "A random forest trains many decision trees on random subsets of data and features, then averages their votes. The crowd of imperfect trees is far more accurate and stable than any single one — a rare case where the ensemble is both stronger and simpler to trust. 🌳", {"keys": ["random forests", "ensemble learning"]}),
    ("a support vector machine", "An SVM finds the boundary that separates classes with the widest margin, and kernel tricks let it draw curved boundaries in higher dimensions. It was the star of machine learning before deep learning, and it still shines on small, clean datasets. 📐", {"keys": ["svm", "support vector machines"]}),
    ("k means clustering", "k-means groups data into k clusters by repeatedly assigning points to the nearest center and moving centers to the average of their points. Fast and intuitive — and it will happily find clusters in pure noise, so the number k deserves skepticism. 🎯", {"keys": ["kmeans", "clustering"]}),
    ("a recommender system", "A recommender system predicts what you will want next — movies, products, songs — from your history and the behavior of similar users. It is one of AI's most economically important applications, and its side effects (filter bubbles, engagement chasing) are now studied as carefully as its accuracy. 🎬", {"keys": ["recommendation system", "recommendations"]}),
    ("a foundation model", "A foundation model is a large model pretrained on broad data that can be adapted to many downstream tasks — GPT, CLIP, and their descendants. The economic shift: train once at great expense, then fine-tune or prompt for thousands of applications. 🏛️", {"keys": ["foundation models"]}),
    ("a multimodal model", "A multimodal model understands more than one kind of data — text plus images, audio, or video. It can describe a photo, answer questions about a chart, or follow spoken instructions, because it learned a shared representation across the modalities. 🎨", {"keys": ["multimodal ai"]}),
    ("quantization", "Quantization stores a model's numbers with fewer bits — 16-bit or 4-bit instead of 32-bit — shrinking memory and speeding up inference at a small accuracy cost. It is the trick that lets large models run on laptops and phones instead of data centers. 📉", {"keys": ["quantized models", "model quantization"]}),
    ("edge ai", "Edge AI runs models on the device — phone, camera, laptop — instead of the cloud. Benefits: latency, privacy, offline operation, no per-call cost. Costs: small models, small batteries, and painful optimization — which is exactly the trade Farell's OpenVoice makes by design. 📱", {"keys": ["on device ai", "on-device machine learning"]}),
    ("the singularity", "The technological singularity is the hypothetical point where AI improves itself faster than humans can follow, making the future fundamentally unpredictable. It is speculation, not scheduled — treat confident date claims about it as entertainment. 🔮", {"keys": ["technological singularity"]}),
]
